{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3878","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3878","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3878\",\"parent_revision\":\"0c28b21ad87c18dfb6da94045126e9da0d5ef6e2ca01922628fac62d799c04ab\",\"root_revision\":\"0c28b21ad87c18dfb6da94045126e9da0d5ef6e2ca01922628fac62d799c04ab\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-24\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n## Desired outcome\\n\\nThe development harness has one supplier-neutral Work Brief capability that normalizes a backlog item, conversation, interview, or brainstorming session into a ready-for-design problem or outcome dataset, then carries it through architecture, Plan design, task decomposition, execution, gates, concerns, and completion. Provider-backed and host-local ad-hoc sources enter the same downstream lifecycle while Work Brief, Plan, and Task remain distinct.\\n\\n## Observed gap and evidence\\n\\nSAM can execute issue-less Plans and local ledgers, but the confirmed current-state evidence shows no unified source-to-Work-Brief seam and no complete local one-off route through the provider-neutral lifecycle. The implementation gaps are independently owned by the dependency records below.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nImmediate ad-hoc work cannot receive the same evidence, readiness, architecture, planning, execution, and verification discipline without first creating remote backlog state or manually bypassing parts of the lifecycle.\\n\\n## Scope boundaries\\n\\nThis parent is coordination only. It does not restate or broaden the acceptance contracts of its dependencies, own implementation tasks, turn a Work Brief into a Plan, or make retention and documentation-authority cleanup block the core capability.\\n\\n## Readiness and acceptance conditions\\n\\nThis parent is complete only when every dependency below is complete and integration evidence demonstrates that supplier-backed and local ad-hoc Work Briefs reach the common ready-for-design and downstream lifecycle without violating provider routing or verification contracts.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nImmediate ad-hoc work cannot receive the same evidence, readiness, architecture, planning, execution, and verification discipline without first creating remote backlog state or manually bypassing parts of the lifecycle.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: User-confirmed grilling state and 2026-09-24 Work Brief documentation design\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-24\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Grooming\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-24T09:01:26.901007Z</sub>\\n\\n# Ad-hoc Work Brief grilling state\\n\\nStatus: complete (shared understanding reconfirmed)\\nUpdated: 2026-09-24\\nPurpose: compaction-safe state for the interactive design of supplier-neutral Work Brief intake and local ad-hoc routing.\\n\\n## Problem being addressed\\n\\nThe development-harness pipeline can execute issue-less plans, but it lacks a supplier-neutral intake path that turns either a provider-backed backlog item or an interactive user conversation into the same ready-for-design input. Ad-hoc work must remain host-local while continuing to use the downstream architecture, planning, task, ledger, concern, gate, and completion contracts.\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nDefine the smallest coherent change that lets a conversation, backlog item, interview, or brainstorming session become a ready-for-design Work Brief, then pass through the existing architecture and SAM planning pipeline without requiring a remote project-management backend.\\n\\n## Settled decisions\\n\\n### Domain model\\n\\n- A **Work Brief** is the supplier-neutral normalized problem or outcome dataset.\\n- Backlog items, interviews, brainstorming sessions, and conversations are sources of a Work Brief.\\n- **Ready for design** means sufficiently defined for architecture, design, and planning to begin.\\n- Do not use \u201cDesign Workbook\u201d or \u201cfully groomed.\u201d\\n- A Work Brief and a Plan are different: a Plan decomposes one Work Brief into executable Tasks.\\n- Disparate concerns become separate dependency-linked Work Briefs, not tasks in one Plan.\\n- Interactive ad-hoc intake scales clarification, discovery, research, brainstorming, feasibility checking, and grilling to the request.\\n- Intake has no fixed form: it is complete when it contains what downstream architecture, planning, and SAM execution need for the specific request, and user-addressable blockers have been resolved.\\n- Before recommending work, verify that it is currently relevant and identify the observable present-day problem it solves.\\n- The Work Brief is created only after the grilling frontier is empty and the user confirms shared understanding.\\n- A separate intake/routing skill owns conversation or context through clarification, brainstorming, grilling, and the architecture handoff. `implement-feature` remains a Plan executor.\\n- After convergence, architecture, Plan design, task decomposition, and implementation use the common contract unattended; user interaction resumes only for genuine blockers or `needs-input` decisions.\\n\\n### Ad-hoc identity and routing\\n\\n- Ad-hoc Work Briefs are host-local, one-off work by default.\\n- The reserved local-overlay identity is `brief~<mandatory-2-3-word-slug>-<4-lowercase-hex>`.\\n- The slug uses lowercase ASCII words, is meaningful and immutable, and is truncated only at word boundaries; there is no fallback slug.\\n- A collision is an error: no overwrite, `force`, or automatic retry. The response contains the colliding reference, existing title, `retryable: false`, guidance to update/groom that reference for the same work, and guidance to invoke creation again for distinct work.\\n- Do not add lower-layer immutability machinery beyond the backlog's existing reference protections.\\n- `brief~` selects the existing SQLite adapter and never falls back to a remote provider.\\n- When SQLite is already the configured backend, ordinary local identifiers remain sufficient; the reserved prefix is needed only to override another configured backend.\\n- Non-`brief~` identifier patterns are documentation and validation metadata only. Project configuration selects the backend for every non-`brief~` reference.\\n- Composite routing covers work items, content/artifacts, plans/tasks, concerns, gates, and completion.\\n- Plan creation atomically records `plan_id -> backend + brief_reference`.\\n- Mixed listings contain remote and local records with routing provenance.\\n- Completed local briefs remain until explicit cleanup; a retention policy is separate follow-up work.\\n- Apart from provider selection, a local brief follows the ordinary backlog lifecycle, including ordinary tags/labels.\\n- `brief~` reuses the existing project-local SQLite backlog database and adapter; it does not introduce a second local store.\\n\\n### Provider creation contract\\n\\n- Generic `backlog add` accepts optional `--reference`.\\n- A provider that supports caller-assigned references accepts it.\\n- A provider that does not support it creates normally, returns its canonical reference, and emits a warning.\\n- SQLite supports caller-assigned references.\\n- Creation and update are separate operations.\\n- Create is create-only; update/groom modifies an exact existing reference.\\n- Minimal response: `{ \\\"reference\\\": \\\"canonical-reference\\\", \\\"warnings\\\": [] }`.\\n- Do not add a duplicated `reference_accepted` field.\\n\\n### Backend status\\n\\n- Add JSON-only `backend status [optional-reference]`.\\n- The CLI resolves configuration/routing, then calls adapter-owned `status()`.\\n- The JSON includes CLI-owned routing/configuration context; adapter-owned runtime status remains under the adapter result.\\n- Online adapters check availability and authentication by default with a 30-second overall timeout.\\n- Exit 0 only when reachable; timeout, authentication, rate limit, and other errors are nonzero and represented in JSON. Backend connection-status error types pass through as structured status rather than being flattened or replaced.\\n- The stable adapter result contains `availability` and an extensible adapter-owned `details` object.\\n- SQLite details may include database path and counts; GitHub details may include authentication state, username, and offline-cache state.\\n- Do not expose secrets, raw environment values, or unsafe exception text.\\n- Keep the initial schema minimal and document the extension point.\\n- `backend status <reference>` reports the adapter selected by that reference; `backend status` reports the configured primary adapter.\\n\\n### Outputs, reports, and artifacts\\n\\n- `artifact_read` with no `artifact_id` returns the artifact list; explicit identity returns content.\\n- Zero matches returns an empty list with count 0; omitted identity always lists, even for one match.\\n- Callers, documentation, and tests migrate atomically; newest-wins was never desired.\\n- An expected output is lightweight: a name and stored-location reference.\\n- Every listed expected output is required.\\n- The worker writes an output, reads it back, checks it against what actually occurred, and records its reference/result.\\n- The judge independently reads and verifies the output before acceptance.\\n- Failed, blocked, and needs-input results record absent outputs and reasons; they do not create placeholders.\\n- CLEAR/CoVe structures tasks and validates task results. Report fidelity separately compares the persisted report with actual actions, observations, outputs, errors, and validation.\\n- All DH roles use `dh:subagent-contract`: transport status is `DONE|BLOCKED`; durable result is `complete|failed|blocked|needs-input`; domain verdict is separate.\\n- Scratch files are not expected outputs. A standalone specialist outside a pipeline may use scratch for specialist-to-orchestrator handoff.\\n- When no item or Plan exists, output persistence is orchestrator-owned: dispatch supplies a parent-controlled destination, or the specialist returns the complete body inline for the orchestrator to store. Agents do not invent child-worktree paths.\\n\\n### Failure disposition\\n\\n- Every non-complete attempt receives a durable disposition: retrying, awaiting user, repairing blocker, backlogged, or accepted as terminal.\\n\\n### Pipeline seam\\n\\n`conversation/backlog source -> normalize Work Brief -> ready-for-design -> architecture -> plan design -> task decomposition -> implement-feature(plan_ref)`\\n\\n- `/dh:work-brief` is the user-invocable ad-hoc entrypoint to an offline, one-off workflow. It follows `/dh:work-backlog-item` while bypassing the configured backend to create a local-only one-off backlog item.\\n- It supports developing features, fixing bugs, or changing documentation in any project without a configured DH backend, and handling work immediately without first filing it in the configured backlog.\\n- The resulting local backlog item is still a Work Brief and follows the normal downstream lifecycle.\\n- Core stages use provider-neutral interfaces.\\n- Normal provider behavior remains; there is no special projection-back phase.\\n\\n### Grilling state contract (Q52)\\n\\n- Every grilling session writes to a scratch-directory state file that the agent reads and updates during grilling.\\n- It preserves settled answers, unanswered questions, evidence, and concerns across turns and compaction.\\n- Every grilling response begins by naming the exact state-file path.\\n- When grilling is complete, the verified state is normalized into the Work Brief.\\n\\n### Delivery order\\n\\n- Update `plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md` with domain terms only, update DH architecture documentation with the normalized Work Brief pipeline, and write the separate normalization/readiness and composite-routing ADRs first.\\n- Review those documentation changes before creating backlog Work Briefs.\\n- Create the dependency-ordered implementation plan after the backlog Work Briefs exist.\\n\\n### Post-confirmation documentation lifecycle conflict\\n\\n- The confirmed docs-first state assigns unimplemented target Work Brief content to current architecture, context, provider, lifecycle, and schema authorities.\\n- `rules/adr-lifecycle.md` requires current authorities to state implemented behavior and ADRs to hold unimplemented deliberation. Those requirements conflict until the target behavior is implemented.\\n- Owner decision: architecture documents define the desired state; ADRs record how and why that state was selected; backlog items track current-to-desired implementation gaps; audits and tests may compare implementation with architecture.\\n- `rules/adr-lifecycle.md` must be corrected because its current-only architecture wording conflicts with this owner decision.\\n- Work Brief desired-state content belongs in the assigned architecture, context, provider, lifecycle, and schema authorities now. Observed implementation gaps belong in backlog records.\\n\\n### Decision records and grilling provenance\\n\\n- Work Brief normalization/readiness and composite local routing are separate ADRs.\\n- After verified conversion, delete the scratch grilling file as a separate authority.\\n- Preserve its substantive content in the Work Brief's `grooming` section.\\n- The persistent logical owner of that preserved grilling provenance is the existing `sections[\\\"groomed\\\"]` / `GroomedData` content layer.\\n- The `grooming` section is the decision/provenance record used during subsequent discovery, architecture, and planning; it does not replace repository ADRs for architecture decisions.\\n- Work Brief ADRs use the issue-independent filename shape `YYYYMMDD-<decision-slug>.md` in `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/`.\\n- ADRs authored on a branch have status `Proposed`; merge confers `Accepted`.\\n\\n### ADR directory instructions\\n\\n- Add one naming-guidance file: `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/AGENTS.md`.\\n- The active production directory is `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/`. Its legacy pattern is `ADR-<tracking-issue>-<per-issue-sequence>-<slug>.md`; the approved new issue-independent pattern is `YYYYMMDD-<decision-slug>.md`.\\n- `.claude/decisions/` is outside this change and must not receive a new `AGENTS.md`.\\n- The evaluation-fixture `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/evaluate-and-tighten-skills/evals/files/adr-worthy/component/docs/adrs/` is not a production ADR directory and is excluded from instruction changes.\\n\\n## Answered questions\\n\\n- Q1: Missing artifact identity returns the list so the correct artifact can be requested without an error-induced extra turn.\\n- Q2: Planner output requirements are part of task completion; errors produce an error report with concerns/observations and do not fabricate outputs.\\n- Q3: Remove the typed-resolver and `SubsectionKey.CONTENT` proposals because no current problem was demonstrated.\\n- Q4\u2013Q6: Omitted artifact identity returns the established list response regardless of cardinality, and every caller, document, and test migrates atomically; newest-wins is rejected.\\n- Q7: Expected outputs communicate state and may be artifact, ledger section, plan/task note, or report; they are not production assets.\\n- Q8: Workers read back their reports and verify fidelity against what they did; this is separate from CLEAR/CoVe prompting.\\n- Q9: State documents are often required outputs; durable task status uses the subagent contract.\\n- Q13\u2013Q15: Failure state is durable, returns to architecture/plan generation where appropriate, and interactive context is gathered from the user.\\n- Q21: Work Brief is the unifying term.\\n- Q24: Ad-hoc work uses the normal SQLite-backed lifecycle; no extra projection phase.\\n- Q26\u2013Q34: Local identity, route, storage, output, retention, conformance, and separate-brief decisions accepted as captured above.\\n- Q36\u2013Q51: Backend probing, identity syntax, provider reference support, collision behavior, response shape, and no-fallback behavior accepted as captured above.\\n- Q52: Per-turn scratch state and conversion into the Work Brief accepted as captured above.\\n- Q53: Reuse the existing project-local SQLite backlog database and adapter.\\n- Q55: Status with a reference reports its routed adapter; status without one reports the configured primary adapter.\\n- Q56: Backend-specific status details come from adapter `status()`.\\n- Q57: Documentation precedes backlog Work Brief creation and planning.\\n- Q58: Record normalization/readiness and composite local routing in separate ADRs.\\n- Q59: Every adapter returns a stable envelope containing `availability` plus an extensible adapter-owned `details` object; the CLI owns routing/configuration display.\\n- Q60: Delete the scratch file after verified conversion, while preserving its substantive state in the Work Brief `grooming` section.\\n- Q61: Name the supplier-neutral user-invocable entrypoint `/dh:work-brief`, with the purpose and lifecycle captured under Pipeline seam.\\n- Q62: Preserve grilling provenance in the existing `sections[\\\"groomed\\\"]` / `GroomedData` content layer.\\n- Q63: Use `YYYYMMDD-<decision-slug>.md` as the issue-independent filename shape in the existing development-harness ADR directory.\\n- Q64: Add naming guidance only at `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/AGENTS.md`; `.claude/decisions/` is outside this change.\\n- Q65: Architecture authorities define desired state, ADRs preserve the selection rationale, backlog items track implementation gaps, and audits/tests may compare implementation with architecture. Correct `rules/adr-lifecycle.md` to conform.\\n- Final confirmation: The user confirmed shared understanding after all design questions were resolved.\\n- Renewed final confirmation: The user's directive to proceed after resolving Q65 reconfirmed shared understanding.\\n\\n## Open frontier\\n\\nNone. The frontier is empty.\\n\\n## Current-state evidence\\n\\n- SAM supports issue-less Plans and local ledger execution, but no current skill owns the full conversation-to-Work-Brief-to-architecture handoff.\\n- No downstream semantic consumer requires a GitHub issue number; current provider coupling is storage/lifecycle addressing for discovery, research, artifacts, codebase analysis, T0/TN gates, concerns, and completion.\\n- Failed/blocked note consumption through judge/reclaim is manual and has no acknowledgement marker.\\n- Current provider APIs expose upsert-like `put_work_item`; SQLite and memory providers overwrite explicit-reference collisions.\\n- Current `artifact_read` omitted-ID behavior selects the newest artifact.\\n- The applicable ADR directory is `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/`; singular `docs/adr/` does not exist at the repository root or under development-harness.\\n- Existing development-harness ADR filenames are issue-derived and provide no issue-independent local filename pattern.\\n\\n## Evidence references\\n\\nPreviously inspected repository sources whose provenance is outside the recovered dialogue:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/ARCHITECTURE.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAIMS-REGISTER.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/dh_core/ledger_spec.py`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/work-ledger/work-loop.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/work-ledger/runner-contract.md`\\n\\nSources named or established in the recovered dialogue:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/clear-cove-task-design/SKILL.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md`\\n- `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260923-open-issue-gap-search.md`\\n- Exact related GitHub issue: `#3423`.\\n- Partial related issues: `#88`, `#994`, `#1527`, `#1857`, `#2143`, `#2182`, `#3099`, `#3102`, `#3106`, `#3123`, `#3305`, `#3449`.\\n\\nPost-confirmation review evidence:\\n\\n- `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260924-work-brief-docs-standards-rereview.md`\\n- `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260924-work-brief-docs-spec-rereview.md`\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- Current provider API exposes upsert-like `put_work_item`; SQLite and memory providers overwrite explicit-reference collisions. Create-only behavior requires a provider-level change.\\n- Current `artifact_read` omitted-ID behavior selects the newest artifact and conflicts with the settled contract.\\n- No current public SAM plan operation exposes internal plan-owned document storage.\\n- Some grooming paths coerce references to integer issue numbers; identifier parsing and backend routing must remain provider-neutral.\\n- Failure notes lack an acknowledgement/consumption marker.\\n\\n## Completion gate\\n\\nGrilling is complete again: Q65 is answered, the frontier is empty, and shared understanding was reconfirmed. Preserve this scratch file until its substantive content has been converted into the Work Brief's `sections[\\\"groomed\\\"]` / `GroomedData` content, read back, and verified to contain every settled decision, evidence reference, and concern without duplication or speculation. Delete this scratch file only after that verification succeeds.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Desired Outcome\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe development harness has one supplier-neutral Work Brief capability that normalizes a backlog item, conversation, interview, or brainstorming session into a ready-for-design problem or outcome dataset, then carries it through architecture, Plan design, task decomposition, execution, gates, concerns, and completion. Provider-backed and host-local ad-hoc sources enter the same downstream lifecycle while Work Brief, Plan, and Task remain distinct.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Readiness And Acceptance Conditions\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThis parent is complete only when every dependency below is complete and integration evidence demonstrates that supplier-backed and local ad-hoc Work Briefs reach the common ready-for-design and downstream lifecycle without violating provider routing or verification contracts.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Scope Boundaries\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThis parent is coordination only. It does not restate or broaden the acceptance contracts of its dependencies, own implementation tasks, turn a Work Brief into a Plan, or make retention and documentation-authority cleanup block the core capability.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Observed Gap And Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nSAM can execute issue-less Plans and local ledgers, but the confirmed current-state evidence shows no unified source-to-Work-Brief seam and no complete local one-off route through the provider-neutral lifecycle. The implementation gaps are independently owned by the dependency records below.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"47aafa8303805b937c3188f08f3a6f3cc41f905a82d55f0e95416bce08ed56c8\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABWnreXw\"}"}